{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310752,"nonce":"Qt+kRGM10gRt3zWvd8xcMa/r/gk=","title":"RESOLVED 2026-06-08 (commit 71ff161). Std.int returning 0 on wasm was a wasmtime API misuse in the host stub, NOT a wasm_backend f64→i32 ...","message":"**Resolved at commit 71ff161 (2026-06-08).**\n\n**Original symptom:** `Std.int(hiddenSize / numHeads) = Std.int(64.0)`\nreturned 0 on wasm. Also `Std.int(262_668_288 / 256)` returned 0\n(diagnosed earlier in the bytes-migration session). I initially\ndocumented the cause as a wasm_backend f64→i32 lowering bug. **That\nwas wrong.**\n\n**Real cause:** wasmtime's `Val::F64(u64)` and `Val::F32(u32)` variants\ncarry the raw IEEE 754 bit pattern, not the float value. The\n`haxe_std_int` host stub in `wasm_runner.rs:1903` was reading them\nas if they were the value:\n\n```rust\n// BUG:\nVal::F64(x) =\u003e *x as i64 as i32,\n```\n\nFor 64.0 the bit pattern is `0x4050_0000_0000_0000`, whose low 32\nbits are 0. Same shape for any nonzero float — `Std.int` always\nreturned the low 32 bits of the raw IEEE encoding.\n\n`haxe_std_string` had the symmetric bug at line 1927: `format!(\"{}\", x)`\nwhere `x` is `\u0026u64` formats the bit pattern as an integer string\n(~4.6e18 for 64.0). Less visible because Haxe trace-concatenation\nof Floats routes through the stdlib's `Std.string(Float)` which\nbypasses this stub.\n\n**Fix:**\n\n```rust\nVal::F64(x) =\u003e f64::from_bits(*x) as i32,\nVal::F32(x) =\u003e f32::from_bits(*x) as i32,\n```\n\nHelper functions `val_f32` / `val_f64` at lines 276 / 284 already do\nthis — these two stubs were the outliers that read the variants\ndirectly.\n\n**How to apply:** Any new wasmtime host stub that takes an `f32` /\n`f64` parameter from wasm code must call `f64::from_bits(*x)` to\nrecover the value. Any stub returning a Float must use\n`Val::F64(x.to_bits())` (already done correctly at `haxe_sys_time`\nline 1990).\n\n**No wasm_backend changes needed.** The backend emits the correct\nextern call; only the host stub's value decode was wrong.\n\n**Workaround now obsolete:** `nue/nue/loader/GGUFReader.hx::blockedSize`\nhas a `nElem \u003e\u003e 8` branch left from when this looked like a backend\nbug. Safe to revert to `Std.int(nElem / blockElems)` once the\nworkaround is removed; left in place for now because it's correct\nand zero-cost. Native MIR is unaffected either way.\n\nVerified by [[perf_wasm_bytes_in_linear_memory_arc]] continuation:\nLlama 3.2 1B GGUF load + ArchRegistry.build (post Codex anon-struct\nfix f7ae53f) + tokenizer build all complete on wasm. Next blocker\nmoves to a separate `indirect call type mismatch` inside the\ngeneration loop — see [[bugs_wasm_indirect_call_type_mismatch_gen]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_wasm_std_int_large_float` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}